 ## Subtopics
 
-### Raw Volume Data Reader with Endianness Handling
-This variant explicitly handles byte-order swapping for 16-bit signed integer (`short`) data, which is common in medical imaging files generated on different architectures [S9, unofficial]. It differs from the base reader by iterating through each voxel, reading two bytes individually, masking them with `0xff` to prevent sign-extension errors, and combining them based on an `isCorrectEndian` flag [S9, unofficial]. Choose this when loading raw CT data that may have been saved with swapped byte order [S9, unofficial].
+### [Voxel data loading and quantization](2478-voxel-data-loading-and-quantization.md)
+This method loads raw binary volume data (typically CT scan slices) into a 3D array and applies thresholding and quantization to reduce precision for rendering. It operates directly on raw binary slices without structured metadata parsing, differing from parsers like [HDF5 JSON (H5J) File Parsing](2922-hdf5-json-h5j-file-parsing.md). Choose it when memory bandwidth is limited and reduced precision is acceptable for rendering.
 
-### Raw Data Parsing and Gradient Computation
-This variant extends the reader by parsing an accompanying metadata text file to extract resolution, sample type, ratio, and endian information, then computes gradients and records min/max intensity values [S10, unofficial]. It differs from the base reader by integrating metadata parsing and gradient calculation into the loading process [S10, unofficial]. Choose this when the raw binary file lacks an internal header and metadata is stored externally [S10, unofficial].
+### [Raw Volume Data Reader with Endianness Handling](12149-raw-volume-data-reader-with-endianness-handling.md)
+This method extends the [Raw Volume Data Reader](2259-raw-volume-data-reader.md) by explicitly handling byte-order mismatches between the source file and the host system. It solves the problem of corrupted voxel values when reading 16-bit signed integer (`short`) data from machines with different endianness (e.g., big-endian files on little-endian x86 hardware). It sets itself apart from generic readers by enforcing a specific byte-combination logic for 16-bit values and guarding against sign-extension errors during byte masking. Choose it when loading raw CT data that may have been saved with swapped byte order [Brief].
 
-### Raw 2D Image Stream Loading
-This variant accepts a list of files containing raw 2D images (voxel streams in row-major order, possibly with a fixed-length header) instead of a single 3D file [S11]. It differs from the base reader by processing multiple 2D slices sequentially and assembling them into a 3D volume [S11]. Choose this when the data is split across multiple files or when processing a stream of 2D slices [S11].
+### [Raw Data Parsing and Gradient Computation](15391-raw-data-parsing-and-gradient-computation.md)
+This method parses raw binary volume data and associated metadata files to initialize the volume representation for rendering. It extracts resolution, data type, and endianness, loads the voxel intensities, and computes per-voxel gradients required for lighting calculations. It is distinguished from sibling loaders by its specific reliance on a custom text-based "info" file format and its explicit handling of gradient computation and transfer function control point parsing within the same loading pipeline. Choose it when the raw binary file lacks an internal header and metadata is stored externally.
+
+### [Raw 2D Image Stream Loading](16441-raw-2d-image-stream-loading.md)
+This method imports a sequence of 2D images into a 3D voxel grid (Voxmap), storing dimensions, metadata, and raw voxel data in separate files. It accepts input from a list of files containing raw 2D images instead of a single 3D TIFF file. Unlike [Voxel data loading and quantization](2478-voxel-data-loading-and-quantization.md), which handles quantization, this method focuses on the structural assembly of a voxel grid from an image stream, potentially preceded by fixed-length headers. Choose it when the data is split across multiple files or when processing a stream of 2D slices.
+
+### [MetaVolume Data Loader and Time-Step Cache](22064-metavolume-data-loader-and-time-step-cache.md)
+A data loading and caching system for volumetric datasets composed of multiple sub-volumes with time steps. It parses a configuration file, reads raw binary volume data, handles data type conversion and endian swapping, and caches previous time steps to avoid re-reading from disk. It builds on [Raw Volume Data Reader](2259-raw-volume-data-reader.md) by adding multi-subvolume support and temporal caching. Choose it for dynamic or time-varying volumetric datasets [Brief].
 
